WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.629
Understanding TCP sliding windows.

2
00:00:03.979 --> 00:00:09.970
The mechanism that lets TCP send data quickly and reliably, without overwhelming the receiver.

3
00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:17.212
Every host taking part in a TCP connection has a send window and a receive window.

4
00:00:17.562 --> 00:00:20.583
Data flows in both directions at the same time.

5
00:00:20.933 --> 00:00:27.065
What host A sends arrives in host B's receive window, and what host B sends arrives in host A's.

6
00:00:29.167 --> 00:00:32.537
In this example, the window size is eight kilobytes.

7
00:00:32.887 --> 00:00:35.990
And the TCP segment size is one kilobyte.

8
00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:41.402
So the sender may transmit eight segments before it must stop and wait for an acknowledgement.

9
00:00:43.533 --> 00:00:47.841
The sender transmits segments one through eight, without waiting between them.

10
00:00:48.191 --> 00:00:52.043
They arrive at the receiver, which places them in its receive window.

11
00:00:52.393 --> 00:00:55.937
All eight are in flight before the first acknowledgement returns.

12
00:00:58.067 --> 00:01:00.143
This is where the window slides.

13
00:01:00.493 --> 00:01:05.069
When the receiver acknowledges segments one and two, the window moves past them.

14
00:01:05.419 --> 00:01:10.623
Now segments nine and ten fall inside the window, and the sender transmits them immediately.

15
00:01:10.973 --> 00:01:15.086
As long as acknowledgements keep arriving, the sender never has to stop.

16
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.838
Now suppose segment two is lost on the way.

17
00:01:20.188 --> 00:01:27.326
The receiver gets segments three, four and five, and holds them, but it can only acknowledge data that arrives in order.

18
00:01:27.676 --> 00:01:30.149
So it keeps acknowledging segment one.

19
00:01:30.499 --> 00:01:38.290
The sender notices, either when a timer expires or after repeated acknowledgements for the same data, and sends segment two again.

20
00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:45.234
The receiver now has one through five, and a single acknowledgement covers them all. The window slides forward again.

21
00:01:47.367 --> 00:01:51.187
Sliding windows give TCP two performance improvements.

22
00:01:51.537 --> 00:01:54.751
They eliminate the time spent waiting for acknowledgements.

23
00:01:55.101 --> 00:01:57.252
And they minimize network traffic.

24
00:01:59.367 --> 00:02:03.177
To see why this matters, look at the simplest possible rule.

25
00:02:03.527 --> 00:02:08.183
Send one segment, then wait for its acknowledgement before sending the next.

26
00:02:08.533 --> 00:02:18.034
It is reliable, but slow. The sender is idle for most of every round trip, and the link carries only one segment per round trip, however fast it is.

27
00:02:20.167 --> 00:02:25.927
With a larger window, the sender keeps transmitting while the first acknowledgements are still on their way back.

28
00:02:26.277 --> 00:02:31.267
Each acknowledgement slides the window and releases more data, so the pipe stays full.

29
00:02:31.617 --> 00:02:42.603
And because acknowledgements are cumulative, one can confirm several segments. Receivers commonly acknowledge every second segment, which cuts acknowledgement traffic roughly in half.

30
00:02:44.733 --> 00:02:51.163
The window is also how the receiver controls the pace. Every acknowledgement carries its current window size.

31
00:02:51.513 --> 00:02:59.202
If the receiving application reads slowly, its buffer fills, the advertised window shrinks, and the sender slows down.

32
00:02:59.552 --> 00:03:05.732
If the window reaches zero, the sender pauses, and sends a small window probe from time to time.

33
00:03:06.082 --> 00:03:09.985
When space opens again, the window reopens and data flows.

34
00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:15.107
So the sliding window delivers both improvements.

35
00:03:15.457 --> 00:03:18.409
No more time lost waiting for each acknowledgement.

36
00:03:18.759 --> 00:03:20.748
And less traffic on the network.

37
00:03:22.867 --> 00:03:30.440
A sliding window lets TCP keep the pipe full, recover from loss without starting over, and match its speed to the receiver.

38
00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:35.379
It is one of the simplest ideas in networking, and one of the most important.
